iOS port of GNU Emacs
=====================

This directory contains the iOS-specific scaffolding for building
Emacs as an iOS application bundle distributable through the App
Store.  The C-side terminal driver, font and pasteboard bridges
live under src/ios*.{m,h,c} and src/iosvfs.c, with Lisp-side
initialization in lisp/term/ios-win.el.  This directory holds:

  Emacs.base/                Bundle template (Info.plist template and
                             launch screen), assembled into the
                             output .app at build time.
  Emacs/                     Objective-C app shell (main.m,
                             AppDelegate, SceneDelegate).  These are
                             linked into the same Mach-O as the Emacs
                             core, and call into ios_main() from
                             src/ios.m once UIKit has finished
                             launching.
  Makefile.in                Bundle-assembly orchestration; mirrors
                             nextstep/Makefile.in's tar + copy +
                             codesign approach.

Building
--------

On a macOS host with Xcode:

  ./autogen.sh
  ./configure --with-ios
  make ios

produces the simulator Emacs.app; install and launch with

  xcrun simctl install booted build-ios-sim/ios/Emacs.app
  xcrun simctl launch booted org.gnu.emacs

See .github/workflows/ios-cross.yml and ios-sim-diag.yml for the
exact CI invocations, including the install / launch / screenshot
smoke test that runs on every push.

TLS (GnuTLS)
------------

Without further preparation the build carries no TLS, so anything
that opens an https connection (package refresh against GNU ELPA,
url.el, network streams with :type tls) fails.  To include TLS,
cross-compile the GnuTLS stack first and point configure at it:

  ios/build-deps.sh --sdk iphoneos --prefix "$PWD/ios-deps/iphoneos"
  ./configure --with-ios --with-ios-deps="$PWD/ios-deps/iphoneos"
  make ios

build-deps.sh fetches, cross-compiles, and installs static GMP,
Nettle, and GnuTLS for the requested SDK (pass --sdk
iphonesimulator for simulator builds -- the deps prefix must match
the SDK the Emacs build targets).  It needs pkg-config on the host
(see "Host tools" below).  Tarballs are downloaded from
ftp.gnu.org / www.gnupg.org over TLS into <prefix>/../downloads;
pre-seed that directory with independently verified tarballs if
your threat model requires it.  The script prints the SHA-256 of
every tarball it uses.

This mirrors the Android port, which bundles cross-compiled
GnuTLS, GMP, Nettle, libtasn1, and p11-kit built with its
ndk-build harness (see java/INSTALL, section GNUTLS).  The iOS
stack is smaller: libtasn1 and unistring are built into GnuTLS
(--with-included-*) and PKCS#11 support is off, so p11-kit is not
needed.

Trust anchors: iOS has no /etc/ssl, so ios/Makefile exports the
build host's Apple root-CA set into the bundle as
etc/ca-bundle.pem (security find-certificate -a -p against
SystemRootCertificates.keychain), and lisp/term/ios-win.el points
gnutls-trustfiles at it.

Licensing: GnuTLS is LGPLv2.1+; Nettle and GMP are dual
GPLv2+/LGPLv3+.  All are compatible with GPLv3+ Emacs.  For App
Store submissions, note that shipping TLS does not change the
export-compliance picture materially: standard HTTPS falls under
the usual exemption categories, but the ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption
declaration (see below) remains the submitter's call.

Host tools (Homebrew or Nix)
----------------------------

Xcode is required either way: the iOS SDKs and the codesigning
machinery come only from Apple, so no package manager replaces it
(and the Command Line Tools alone are not enough -- check that
xcode-select -p points into Xcode.app).  The build needs four
host tools beyond Xcode: autoconf, automake, texinfo (for
makeinfo), and pkg-config.

Homebrew:

  brew install autoconf automake texinfo pkg-config

Nix: prefer `nix shell', which only puts the tools on PATH:

  nix shell nixpkgs#autoconf nixpkgs#automake \
            nixpkgs#texinfo nixpkgs#pkg-config

Avoid building from a `nix-shell -p' / stdenv dev shell: those
export CC, CXX, LDFLAGS, and NIX_CFLAGS_COMPILE pointing at Nix's
own clang wrapper and SDK, and the outer (host) configure pass
would pick them up in preference to the Xcode toolchain.  If you
must work inside such a shell, unset CC CXX LD CPPFLAGS CFLAGS
LDFLAGS NIX_CFLAGS_COMPILE NIX_LDFLAGS before running configure.
The cross side is pinned regardless: ios/build-deps.sh resolves
clang, ar, ranlib, and strip through xcrun, and the recursive
XCONFIGURE=ios pass gets CC/CXX/AR/RANLIB the same way from
cross/xcode-build/xcode-toolchain.sh, so Nix toolchains on PATH
cannot leak into the iOS binaries; the same applies to
pkg-config, which only ever
reads the .pc files inside the --with-ios-deps prefix
(PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR is forced there, so a Nix store pkg-config
cannot pull host libraries into the cross link).

Installing on a physical device
-------------------------------

`xcrun simctl install booted ...` is simulator-only.  For a real
device you cross-configure against the iphoneos SDK, sign with a
provisioning-profile-matched entitlements plist, and install with
`xcrun devicectl` (Xcode 15+).  The Makefile has three optional
variables that together drive this:

  CODESIGN_IDENTITY    name of the signing identity in the keychain
  PROVISIONING_PROFILE path to a .mobileprovision file
  DEVICE_ENTITLEMENTS  path to the entitlements plist for codesign

End-to-end recipe:

  # 1. Cross-configure for the iphoneos SDK (not iphonesimulator).
  #    The bundle id MUST be one registered to your Apple Developer
  #    team -- you cannot sign org.gnu.emacs with your own
  #    certificates.
  IOS_SDK=iphoneos ./configure --with-ios \
    --with-ios-deployment-target=15.0 \
    --with-ios-bundle-id=com.yourteam.emacs

  # 2. Extract the profile's Entitlements dict as your starting
  #    entitlements file.  The Makefile target shells out to
  #    `security cms -D -i` (QA1798) and PlistBuddy.
  make -C ios device-entitlements-template \
    PROVISIONING_PROFILE=~/Library/MobileDevice/Provisioning\ Profiles/UUID.mobileprovision \
    > device.entitlements

  # 3. Build, embed the profile, sign in one shot.
  make ios \
    CODESIGN_IDENTITY="Apple Development: NAME (TEAMID)" \
    PROVISIONING_PROFILE=~/Library/MobileDevice/Provisioning\ Profiles/UUID.mobileprovision \
    DEVICE_ENTITLEMENTS=$PWD/device.entitlements

  # 4. Install and launch on the device.
  xcrun devicectl device install app --device <UDID> \
    build-ios/ios/Emacs.app
  xcrun devicectl device process launch --console --device <UDID> \
    org.gnu.emacs

`IOS_SDK=iphoneos` makes configure.ac substitute iPhoneOS into
Info.plist's `CFBundleSupportedPlatforms` / `DTPlatformName`;
the device rejects bundles whose Info.plist still says
iPhoneSimulator.

Three on-device prerequisites that are easy to miss:

  * Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Developer Mode must be
    toggled on (iOS 16+; restart and passcode confirm).  Without
    it, devicectl install fails with "Developer Mode disabled".
  * Settings -> General -> VPN & Device Management -> Trust your
    team.  Required once per signing certificate the device has
    never seen, for both free Personal Team and paid Developer
    Program certs.
  * The device's UDID must be in the profile's
    `ProvisionedDevices` and the signing cert must be in its
    `DeveloperCertificates`.

What the build system actually does, with citations:

  * Profile is copied to `$(ios_appdir)/embedded.mobileprovision`
    BEFORE codesign runs.  Apple's published signing engine
    (apple-oss-distributions/Security, libsecurity_codesigning,
    bundlediskrep.cpp `defaultResourceRules`) seals every bundle
    file under a V2 catch-all rule; modifying the profile after
    signing invalidates the seal.
  * codesign is invoked with `--entitlements $(DEVICE_ENTITLEMENTS)`
    when set.  The same engine source (signer.cpp `prepare` /
    `populate` / `cookEntitlements`) writes that plist verbatim
    into slot 5 plus a DER copy in slot 7.  No merging with the
    profile happens; the profile is never opened by codesign.
  * The plist must be a subset of the profile's `Entitlements`
    dict (Apple TN3125's allowlist rule).  The
    device-entitlements-template target gives you the profile's
    dict as a starting point; for a minimal app it is also the
    final file.

Support framing.  This is the same approach every off-Xcode iOS
toolchain (Unity, Unreal, Flutter, Godot, Qt) uses, for the same
reason -- the codesign + security + devicectl primitives are
individually Apple-documented, but Apple does not publish an
iOS-specific end-to-end recipe.  Apple's documented stance
(Quinn DTS, forum thread 797751: "Investigating Third-Party IDE
Code-Signing Problems") is that DTS does not support third-party
toolchains for iOS signing; the supported path is to wrap the
build in an Xcode project and use `xcodebuild archive` +
`xcodebuild -exportArchive -exportOptionsPlist`.

Diagnostic recipe if devicectl install fails.  The error wraps
`com.apple.dt.CoreDeviceError Code=3002` around a
`MobileInstallationService` code in its underlyingError:

  0xe8008015  UDID not in profile, profile expired, or
              application-identifier mismatch
  0xe8008016  entitlement claimed by binary is not in profile
              allowlist
  0xe800801a  profile signature invalid / untrusted
  0xe800801c  no code signature found

Inspect the signed entitlements with `codesign -d --entitlements
:- App.app` and the profile with `security cms -D -i
App.app/embedded.mobileprovision`; compare `application-identifier`,
`TeamIdentifier`, `ProvisionedDevices`, and every entitlement key.

If codesign itself fails with errSecInternalComponent, the
Security framework could not use the signing key: the login
keychain is locked (typical after a reboot, and always over SSH)
or the key's access control does not authorize codesign
non-interactively.  Unlock with

  security unlock-keychain login.keychain-db

and, if it recurs, authorize the key permanently:

  security set-key-partition-list -S apple-tool:,apple: \
    -k <login-password> login.keychain-db

devicectl needs Xcode 15+ and iOS 17+.  Older targets need
ios-deploy (abandoned for iOS 17+) or pymobiledevice3.  The
`--console` flag for streaming the app's stdout/stderr requires
Xcode 16; with Xcode 15 use `log stream --device <UDID>` or
Console.app instead.

Packaging an .ipa for TestFlight / App Store upload
---------------------------------------------------

  make ios-ipa \
    CODESIGN_IDENTITY="Apple Distribution: NAME (TEAMID)" \
    PROVISIONING_PROFILE=path/to/AppStoreProfile.mobileprovision \
    DEVICE_ENTITLEMENTS=$PWD/dist.entitlements

writes build-ios/ios/Emacs.ipa -- a ditto-produced zip whose top
level is Payload/Emacs.app, the container App Store Connect
ingestion expects.  For store submission the signing identity
must be an Apple Distribution certificate and the profile an App
Store profile (their Entitlements dicts carry no get-task-allow
and do carry beta-reports-active, which is what enables
TestFlight -- QA1830; the device-entitlements-template target
works on them the same way).

Apple's documented upload paths (App Store Connect Help, "Upload
builds"): the Transporter app, xcrun altool --upload-app (still
supported for uploads; only its notarization role was retired in
favor of notarytool), xcodebuild -exportArchive with an upload
destination (Xcode 15+), or the App Store Connect "Build
uploads" REST API.

App Store submission requirements this port already carries
------------------------------------------------------------

  * Privacy manifest.  PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy ships in the bundle
    template and lands at the bundle root.  Mandatory since
    May 1, 2024 for apps using Apple's "required reason" APIs
    (developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/
    describing-use-of-required-reason-api); ingestion rejects
    submissions with undeclared use.  Emacs declares three
    categories: FileTimestamp (stat/fstat everywhere; reasons
    C617.1 container files, 3B52.1 picker-granted files, DDA9.1
    dired display), DiskSpace (statvfs via file-system-info;
    85F4.1, dired's free-space header), UserDefaults (bookmark
    persistence; CA92.1).  If you add code touching a new
    required-reason API category, extend the manifest.
  * Launch screen.  UILaunchStoryboardName + storyboard, required
    for all submissions since mid-2020.
  * App icon.  NOT shipped -- Apple requires original artwork.
    Either drop a 1024x1024 PNG at
    ios/Emacs.base/Assets.xcassets/AppIcon.appiconset/AppIcon.png
    or keep the artwork outside the tree and pass its path on the
    make command line:

      make ios IOS_APP_ICON=$HOME/art/emacs-icon.png

    Both routes compile the icon with actool (single-size asset
    catalog, auto-scaled variants) and merge CFBundleIconName /
    CFBundleIcons into Info.plist.  The PNG must be exactly
    1024x1024 and fully opaque (no alpha channel) for App Store
    acceptance.  Store validation rejects icon-less binaries
    (ITMS-90704 family); Simulator / device development works
    fine without.
  * App name.  Pass --with-ios-app-name=NAME at configure time to
    set the label under the home-screen icon
    (CFBundleDisplayName) and the short name system UI uses
    (CFBundleName; keep it 15 characters or fewer).  Defaults to
    Emacs.  The bundle directory (Emacs.app) and executable name
    are internal and unaffected.  Note the App Store product name
    shown on the store page is set in App Store Connect, not in
    the bundle.
  * Bundle identifier.  Pass --with-ios-bundle-id= at configure
    time; the App Store re-signs on ingestion and the published
    app carries no embedded provisioning profile (TN3125).

Review-policy risks to weigh before submitting:

  * Downloaded code (guideline 2.5.2).  package-install fetches
    Emacs Lisp from ELPA and executes it, and eval runs anything
    the user types.  Apple's guideline permits self-contained
    apps and carves out limited room for educational and
    development tools; interpreter apps exist on the store under
    that reading, but acceptance is reviewer-dependent.  Framing
    the app as a text editor / development environment, keeping
    package installation strictly user-initiated (it is), and
    being ready to appeal are the practical mitigations; the
    fallback is disabling package-install in a store build.
  * UIKit scene lifecycle.  The app uses the legacy
    UIApplicationDelegate launch path (no
    UIApplicationSceneManifest).  Apple has announced that a
    future SDK will require the UIScene lifecycle; when the
    Xcode toolchain in use starts enforcing it, the delegate
    bring-up in src/ios.m needs porting to a scene delegate.
    Not a rejection today, but a known horizon.
  * Reviewer interaction: commands that would spawn subprocesses
    (M-x shell, compile) signal a clean Lisp error on device
    rather than crashing, which is what guideline 2.1 cares
    about.

Handled mechanically by the build (no action needed): privacy
manifest covering FileTimestamp, DiskSpace, UserDefaults, and
SystemBootTime; DT* / BuildMachineOSBuild toolchain-provenance
keys App Store ingestion expects from non-Xcode uploads; compiled
launch storyboard; UIDeviceFamily; asset-catalog icon wiring; and
a payload free of symlinks and stray executables (both are
ingestion rejections).

Submission items that remain YOUR decision:

  * Export compliance.  ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption is not set
    in Info.plist, so App Store Connect will walk you through the
    export-compliance questionnaire on each upload.  With the
    GnuTLS stack included, the app uses encryption in the
    standard HTTPS sense Apple's "Complying with Encryption
    Export Regulations" describes as exempt from documentation
    requirements, but the declaration is a legal statement the
    submitter must make.
  * Licensing.  GNU Emacs is GPLv3.  The FSF's position on App
    Store terms led to the removal of GNU Go (2010) and VLC's
    original takedown.  Nothing technical in this port changes
    that calculus; TestFlight and personal-device installs are
    unaffected.

Every launch bootstraps the Lisp environment from the bundled
sources (the cross build cannot pre-dump, and no dump is written
on device); startup takes a few seconds depending on hardware.
Caching a pdumper image in the sandbox on first launch is the
natural future optimization.

Runtime / threading model
-------------------------

Emacs runs on a dedicated pthread with a 16 MB stack; UIKit owns
the main thread.  The two sides communicate exclusively through:

  * lock-protected queues (keystrokes, rich input events, pinch
    updates) drained by ios_read_socket on the Emacs thread;
  * publish/drain channels for resize, mouse motion, appearance
    changes, and open-file requests: the UIKit side writes plain C
    data under a mutex and pokes a wake pipe; the Emacs thread
    builds any Lisp data on its own side;
  * dispatch_async / dispatch_sync onto the main queue for UIKit
    work initiated from Lisp (clipboard, tooltips, keyboard
    show/hide, pickers, menus).

Invariants: the UIKit thread never allocates Lisp data and never
reads frame state (input events carry a nil frame; the drain
attaches one); the Emacs thread never sleeps on UIKit -- modal UI
(menus, dialogs) runs a nested input pump so C-g and type-ahead
keep working.

HOME and the working directory are the sandboxed Documents/
folder (the one visible in the Files app); .emacs.d is created
there.  All geometry is in logical points (Core Graphics applies
the Retina scale underneath), and the display reports 163 ppi so
the face engine's point<->pixel conversions agree with the
drawing coordinate space.

Feature status (each verified by the CI smoke test when landed)
----------------------------------------------------------------

Works:
  * Text rendering with face attributes: colors, bold, italic,
    underline (single and wave), overline, strike-through, cursor
    styles; per-(size, weight, slant) font caching.
  * Input: hardware keyboards including modifiers, arrows and
    F-keys; the software keyboard with an accessory bar (Esc,
    sticky Ctrl/Meta, Tab, C-g, arrow keys, M-x); tap as mouse-1,
    long-press as mouse-2, single-finger drag as selection with
    live highlight, two-finger pan as wheel events, pinch as
    pinch events (text-scale-pinch).
  * Popup menus (x-popup-menu, mouse-3) as chained action sheets
    with submenu hierarchy, separators, and toggle/radio state;
    dialogs (x-popup-dialog, use-dialog-box) as modal alerts.
  * Tooltips (x-show-tip / x-hide-tip) as an auto-hiding overlay.
  * Clipboard via UIPasteboard (changeCount-cached), wired to
    interprogram-cut/paste-function.
  * Files: open-in-place from other apps via drag-n-drop events;
    ios-pick-file presents the Files picker asynchronously;
    security-scoped bookmarks persist access across launches; the
    save round-trip into Documents is exercised by CI end to end.
  * Frame parameters (colors, cursor, font via
    set_new_font_hook); rotation / split-view / soft-keyboard
    resizes; live dark/light appearance tracking; haptic bell;
    background lifecycle quiesces drawing; the minibuffer
    auto-shows the soft keyboard (defcustom
    ios-auto-show-keyboard).
  * Image loading via ImageIO and Core Graphics
    (iosimage.m); PNG, JPEG, GIF, TIFF, HEIC, BMP without
    third-party dependencies, decoded on the Emacs thread and
    cached through the standard image-cache.
  * Self-test battery (lisp/term/ios-tests.el, M-x
    ios-run-self-tests): twenty-one probes covering sandbox
    identity, file round-trip, clipboard, geometry, appearance,
    image load, face attributes, color-name resolution, theme
    face-spec matching, frame parameters, frame-background-mode,
    the
    pinch / wheel / drag-n-drop event bindings, TLS (GnuTLS
    availability and trust anchors), and documentation (DOC
    docstrings and Info manuals).  One PASS / FAIL line per
    probe is written to ~/ios-test-results.txt.

Known issues / deferred:
  * Rendering uses an overpaint command list (correct, simple);
    per-row damage tracking is the planned optimization.
  * Pane headers inside submenus are dropped -- action sheets
    have no section concept.
  * Subprocesses (make-process / call-process) work in the
    simulator but signal a Lisp error on device: the iOS sandbox
    forbids posix_spawn.  Inherent to the platform.
  * Device signing / TestFlight packaging needs Apple credentials
    and is not exercised by CI.

App Store compliance notes
--------------------------

* No JIT (no native compilation, no dynamic module loading).
* No fork/exec; Emacs subprocess primitives error out on device.
* Sandboxed filesystem; user files live in the per-app Documents
  directory and Files-app picked URLs (security-scoped, bookmark
  persistence across launches).
* pdumper image is data, not executable code, and is generated on
  first launch into the writable Application Support directory.

Debug facilities
----------------

Set these in the app's environment (SIMCTL_CHILD_* under simctl):

  EMACS_IOS_DEBUG_LOG=1   show the launch-log strip above the canvas
  EMACS_IOS_AUTO_INPUT=1  run the CI keystroke driver after launch

Logs land in Documents/emacs-launch.log and emacs-stdout.log.
